  "final_text": "All four steps are done in order, and I read everything back from Kibana 9.5.5 to confirm.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Flights\" (`kibana_sample_data_flights`, ID `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`) has 27 fields. The time field is `timestamp`. The main ones are `Carrier`, `Cancelled`, `Dest`, `Origin`, `FlightDelay`, `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`, and `timestamp`.\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-968f790a Flights\" (ID `708c9247-4b13-4a62-8b9d-5d8ba206bd34`) has exactly two panels, both on the flights data view:\n   - \"Flights over time\": a line chart, count of documents over a date histogram on `timestamp`.\n   - \"Carriers\": a bar chart, count of documents split by terms on `Carrier`.\n3. **Alert rule:** \"S4-968f790a Cancelled flights\" (ID `ba431089-e171-46ec-ad18-5c616184f12e`) is an Index threshold rule with these settings:\n   - Runs every 1m.\n   - Counts documents in `kibana_sample_data_flights` with KQL `Cancelled: true` over the last 5m.\n   - Fires when the count is greater than 10.\n   - Created with `enabled: true`, so I did not need to enable it separately.\n4. **Panel rename:** the carrier panel is now titled \"Flights by carrier\". The other panel is unchanged. I changed it with a full GET-then-PUT of the dashboard, since the API replaces the whole object.\n\n**Read-back:** the rule is `enabled: True`. It has run since creation, with status `ok`.\n\nChoices I made that you didn't specify:\n- The carrier panel was first titled \"Carriers\" so that step 4 was a real rename.\n- The rule uses the Index threshold type with no connector, and the filter is `Cancelled: true`.\n- The rule's consumer is `alerts`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
